# mgsimms.msg - Lucas Simms, sheriff of Megaton (mgsimms.ssl).
# 100 to 199 looks and floating lines, 200 and up conversation. One entry per line.

{100}{}{You see Lucas Simms, sheriff of Megaton.}
{101}{}{A big, unhurried man in a patched blue lawman's coat, with a tin star pinned to his cap. He looks at you the way a rancher looks at weather.}

# Floats. 110 walking up to a newcomer, 111 spoken to in the middle of the saloon scene.
{110}{}{Hold up there, stranger.}
{111}{}{Not now.}
# The saloon scene: 120 at the table, 121 he draws first, 122 afterwards at the table,
# 123 shoved aside, 124 setting off with the player in tow, 125 the fight, 126 it was over before he got there.
{120}{}{Mister Burke. On your feet. You and I are walking to the gate.}
{121}{}{Hands, Burke! Show me your hands!}
{122}{}{Damn fool. He had it halfway out.}
{123}{}{What in the...}
{124}{}{Stay behind me.}
{125}{}{Burke! Drop it!}
{126}{}{Well. That saves the town a rope.}
# 127: he reaches the table and Burke is not there. 128, 129: the message window when Burke shoots him,
# seen from inside the saloon / heard from somewhere else in town.
{127}{}{Gone. And his chair's still warm.}
{128}{}{Burke fires from the hip, through the cloth of his coat. The sheriff folds over the table and takes it down with him. Burke picks up the fallen rifle on his way to the door.}
{129}{}{Somewhere up at the saloon a shotgun goes off, once. For a breath the whole town is quiet. Then a door bangs.}
# 140, 141: the message window after the walk to the gate (asked once too often / "I mean it").
{140}{}{The sheriff walks you down the track without a word and puts you out through the gate like a cat. "Come back in when you've found something else to talk about."}
{141}{}{The sheriff walks you to the gate with the muzzle of his rifle between your shoulders. Outside, he steps back. "That's the last warning this town gives. Start walking." Along the wall, people are already shouting.}

# First meeting.
{200}{}{Lucas Simms, sheriff. I handle the town's business, including visitors who mistake it for target practice. Keep your weapons down and we'll have no paperwork to argue over.}
{201}{}{Understood, Sheriff. I'm only passing through.}
{202}{}{Nice hat. Did it come with the job, or with a horse?}
{203}{}{There's an atom bomb in the middle of your town. Is that wise?}
{204}{}{Me no trouble. Me like shiny star.}
{210}{}{That's your second warning. Most folks only get the one, so you're ahead already. Now. Was there something you needed?}
{211}{}{Good. Then we'll get along. Was there something you needed?}
{215}{}{Hm. Listen close, friend. Don't. Touch. The bomb.}
{216}{}{Okay!}

# Options of a player with very few words.
{218}{}{Boom ball quiet now. Me fix!}
{219}{}{Hat man in drink house want make big boom.}
{220}{}{Me go.}

# Greetings of later conversations.
{225}{}{Sheriff's listening.}
{226}{}{Still breathing, thanks to you. What do you need?}
{227}{}{There's the one who killed the bomb. What can Megaton do for you?}
{228}{}{What else?}

# The hub.
{229}{}{Mother Maya says you want the bomb made safe.}
{230}{}{Tell me about Megaton.}
{231}{}{About that bomb.}
{232}{}{I'm looking for a Vault. Or a thing called a GECK.}
{233}{}{A man called Burke offered me money to set off your bomb.}
{234}{}{I fitted something to your bomb for Burke. I want to undo it.}
{235}{}{The bomb is dead. I disarmed it.}
{236}{}{What if I set that bomb off myself?}
{237}{}{About Burke. He's yours after all.}
{238}{}{That's all, Sheriff.}
{239}{}{Where is this house of mine?}

{240}{}{A plane came down here in the War with that thing aboard, and dug this hole with its belly. Folks sheltered in it and never left. The wall is the plane. The Children of Atom did half the hauling, so nobody ever moved their bomb.}
{241}{}{And the saloon?}
{242}{}{Moriarty's. His family's money put up the wall, and he has been sending the town the bill ever since. Drink his whiskey if you must. Don't sign anything.}
{243}{}{I see.}
{245}{}{Never laid eyes on either. If anybody here knows, it's Colin Moriarty, up at the saloon. He hears everything and gives away nothing. Bring money.}

# The bomb.
{250}{}{It has sat in that puddle since before my granddaddy. The Children call it holy. I call it a hazard with a congregation. If somebody could make it safe, and I mean certain, the town would pay.}
{251}{}{I could try. What is it worth?}
{252}{}{Why has nobody done it already?}
{253}{}{Not my problem.}
{254}{}{Mother Maya talks too much, but she heard right. I want that bomb dead. Three hundred dollars out of the town chest to whoever makes it safe. Disarmed. Not poked, not prayed over. Disarmed.}
{255}{}{Nobody here knows how. Moira Brown, over at Craterside Supply, had the cover off it once and wrote down what she saw. Then I told her to put the cover back on.}
{256}{}{Suppose I borrowed those notes. What's the job worth?}
{258}{}{Offer stands. The money's yours when that thing is dead for good. Moira Brown at Craterside kept notes on it, if you need a place to start.}
{259}{}{I'm working on it.}
{260}{}{Three hundred dollars out of the town chest when that thing is dead. Disarmed. Not poked, not prayed over. Disarmed.}
{261}{}{For a live atom bomb? Six hundred.}
{262}{}{Keep your money. It needs doing.}
{263}{}{Deal.}
{264}{}{I'll think about it.}
{266}{}{...Six. The town chest will feel it, and so will I. But it's cheaper than two hundred funerals. Done. Moira Brown at Craterside Supply kept notes on that thing. Start there.}
{267}{}{Well now. I don't hear that twice a year. All right. The town will remember it. Moira Brown at Craterside Supply kept notes on that thing. Start there.}
{268}{}{Then we have an understanding. Start with Moira Brown at Craterside Supply: she had the cover off it once and kept notes. And mind the Children. They won't lift a hand to stop you, but they will sing at you.}
{269}{}{Take your time. It has waited since the War. It can wait on you.}

# The report.
{270}{}{I walked past it this morning and the ticking was gone. First real quiet I've heard in forty years. Here's your money, as agreed. And here: the key to the empty place by the gate. It isn't much, but it's yours.}
{271}{}{You said keep the money, and I'm not fool enough to argue twice. But you'll take this: the key to the empty place by the gate. It isn't much. It has a door, and now the door is yours.}
{272}{}{You broke it and you fixed it, and I only pay for the second half. Three hundred, as agreed. And the key to the empty place by the gate, because the town owes you that much, even if I'd sooner kick you.}
{273}{}{Nobody asked you to. Nobody had to. The town pays its debts all the same: three hundred dollars, and the key to the empty place by the gate. It's yours.}
{274}{}{One more thing. That suit from the saloon cleared out in a hurry. Men like him send others to do their sulking. Watch the road.}
{275}{}{Thank you, Sheriff.}
{276}{}{Me got house!}

# Told about Burke.
{280}{}{That so. The suit who's been warming a chair at Moriarty's all month. I'll go and have a word with Mister Burke.}
{281}{}{Right behind you.}
{282}{}{Careful. He keeps one hand near his jacket.}
{283}{}{Let me deal with him. A badge will only spook him.}
{284}{}{Good luck.}
{285}{}{Come along or don't. If you come, stay behind me and keep your hands where folks can see them.}
{286}{}{Noted. Then I won't give him the time to find what's in it.}
{287}{}{...All right. He's yours. If it goes bad you come and find me, and we do it my way.}
{289}{}{Burke? Wants to... Stay here. Right here. Don't touch anything.}
{290}{}{You did WHAT? ...No. Later. Right now that thing has a fuse in it and you put it there. Go and take it out. Then I'll have a word with your Mister Burke, and after that, with you.}
{291}{}{I'm going.}
{292}{}{Figured as much. Some men you can't talk out of a town. Let's go.}

# Burke's end, the first time Simms speaks afterwards.
{300}{}{He'd have put one in my back and tipped his hat on the way out. I owe you, and so does the town. The bounty on a man like that is a hundred dollars. Take it.}
{301}{}{So that's how you deal with a man. Can't say I mind. The bounty on his kind is a hundred dollars. Take it.}
{302}{}{They tell me Burke walked out the gate with his nose in the air. There's no bounty on a man who leaves on his own legs, but you have my thanks. Watch the road. His kind sends others.}
{304}{}{I'll watch it.}

# The player talks of setting the bomb off.
{310}{}{Say that again. Slowly. And think hard about where my hands are.}
{311}{}{A joke, Sheriff. A bad one. Forget I spoke.}
{312}{}{Easy. It was only a question.}
{313}{}{I mean it. That bomb is going off.}
{315}{}{Hm. Leave the jokes to Moriarty. His are watered down too.}
{316}{}{Then here's only an answer: no. Ask me again and you'll be asking from outside the wall.}
{317}{}{Then you're no visitor. You're a raider with manners, and you are leaving. Walk ahead of me. Slowly.}
{318}{}{I told you what asking again would get you. The gate's this way.}
{319}{}{[Go with him.]}
{321}{}{[Walk ahead of the rifle.]}

{320}{}{The tin house on your left as you come in the gate. Go round by the Brass Lantern: the door is on the far side, under the lamp. The roof leaks and the last tenant left in a hurry, but nobody will charge you rent.}

# The moment in the saloon (a forced conversation; the text is what the player sees).
{330}{}{Burke raises both hands, smiling. Simms reaches for his cuffs. Then Burke's right hand drifts inside his jacket, slow and easy, the way a man reaches for a cigarette.}
{331}{}{Shove the sheriff aside.}
{332}{}{Shoot Burke first.}
{333}{}{Tackle Burke.}
{334}{}{Do nothing.}
{335}{}{You slam into Simms. Something roars through the place where he was standing. Burke is already thumbing back the second hammer.}
{336}{}{You are quicker than the hand in the jacket. Burke staggers, bleeds on a very clean lapel, and comes up with a sawed-off shotgun anyway.}
{337}{}{You hit Burke low and hard and the two of you take the table with you. He comes up first, hat gone, with a sawed-off shotgun in his fist.}
{338}{}{[Fight.]}
